:\n\n**Two main scripts:**\n\n1. **`/root/setup-git-server.sh`** — Creates the bare repository and post-receive hook\n   - Sets up `/git/server` as a bare git repo\n   - Creates `/var/www/server` as the web root\n   - Installs a post-receive hook that auto-deploys on push\n\n2. **`/root/start-web-server.sh`** — Runs a simple HTTP server on port 8080\n   - Serves files from `/var/www/server`\n   - Uses Python's built-in HTTP server (no dependencies)\n\n**To use it:**\n\n```bash\n# On your server machine, run once:\nbash /root/setup-git-server.sh\n\n# In a separate terminal, start the web server:\nbash /root/start-web-server.sh\n\n# From your client, clone and push as normal:\ngit clone user@server:/git/server my-project\ncd my-project\necho \"hello world\" > hello.html\ngit add hello.html\ngit commit -m \"add hello.html\"\ngit push origin master\n\n# Then verify:\ncurl http://server:8080/hello.html\n```\n\nThe post-receive hook automatically checks out the latest commit to `/var/www/server` whenever you push, so your web server always serves the latest code. See `/root/GIT_SERVER_README.md` for detailed docs and optional enhancements (systemd service, nginx, branch filtering, etc.).","ttft_ms":4989,"type":"result","duration_ms":27010,"uuid":"dbee24b4-e871-4bd6-87f0-3928dec8a137","ttft_stream_ms":866,"time_to_request_ms":44,"queued_turn_count":0}
